(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["context",{"l:context/from-prompt-engineering-to-context-engineering":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Context engineering\u003c/b> is deciding, on every model call, which tokens go into the context window: instructions, tool definitions, memory, retrieved data and conversation history. Prompt engineering (writing good instructions) is one part of it. The goal is the smallest set of high-signal tokens that makes the right output likely, because every extra token costs money, adds latency and dilutes the model's attention.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why the name changed\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In 2023 most LLM work was a single call: write a prompt, get an answer. Getting the wording right was most of the job, hence \"prompt engineering\". By 2025 the typical system was a loop: an assistant with memory, or an agent that calls tools for 20 to 50 steps. In a loop, the prompt you wrote is a small, fixed part of what the model reads. The rest is assembled at runtime: tool results, retrieved documents, notes from earlier sessions and the conversation so far.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Anthropic defines context engineering as the set of strategies for curating and maintaining the optimal set of tokens during inference, including everything that lands in the window besides the prompt. Andrej Karpathy's analogy helps: the model is a CPU and the context window is its RAM. The model can only work with what is loaded right now, and your application is the operating system that decides what to load.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The attention budget\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Why not load everything the model might need? Three reasons.\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Quality.\u003c/b> In a transformer every token attends to every other token, so n tokens create n² relationships to weigh. As n grows, the model's focus is stretched, and models see fewer very long sequences during training. Accuracy degrades gradually as context grows, not at a cliff. Anthropic calls this a finite \u003cb>attention budget\u003c/b>; the measured effect is called \u003cb>context rot\u003c/b> (lesson 4).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost.\u003c/b> Every input token is billed on every call, and a stateless chat re-sends its whole history each turn.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Latency.\u003c/b> The model must process (prefill) every input token before it writes the first output token.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Context in an agent loop\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>user task\n    |\n    v\nassemble context: instructions, tools, memory, history  &lt;-----------+\n    |                                                                |\n    v                                                                |\nmodel call ---&gt; tool call? --- yes ---&gt; run tool, append result -----+\n                    |\n                    no\n                    v\n                 answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Manus, a general-purpose agent product, reports that a typical task takes about 50 tool calls and that its input-to-output token ratio is about 100:1. The context is rebuilt 50 times, and each time the output is a short tool call. That is why agent quality and cost are dominated by context decisions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Four moves\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Move\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it means\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003cth>Where in this guide\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Write\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Save information outside the window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Notes file, memory store, progress log\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Memory module\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Select\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pull the right information in when needed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Search tool, retrieval, opening one file\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Lessons 5 and 9\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Compress\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Keep the meaning in fewer tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Summarise old turns, trim tool output\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Lesson 6, Memory module\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Isolate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Split work across separate windows\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Subagents, sandboxed tool execution\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Lesson 8\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The write, select, compress and isolate split comes from LangChain's survey of how popular agents manage context; it is a handy checklist when a context is too big or too noisy.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How bad context fails\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Missing:\u003c/b> the needed fact is not there, so the model guesses.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Distracting:\u003c/b> relevant-looking but wrong material pulls the answer off course.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Confusing:\u003c/b> many similar tools or documents make the right choice ambiguous.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Conflicting:\u003c/b> an old instruction or fact contradicts a newer one and the model picks one arbitrarily.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Poisoned:\u003c/b> an earlier hallucination, or a malicious instruction inside a tool result, enters the history and is treated as true on every later turn.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Strong teams treat the context as a build artefact. They log the full rendered request for every call (system prompt, tool schemas, every message), count tokens per section, and read real traces when something goes wrong. A useful test: if a capable new colleague were handed exactly this context and nothing else, could they do the task? Coding agents expose this view directly; Claude Code's \u003ccode>/context\u003c/code> command, for example, shows how the window splits between system prompt, tools, memory files and messages. Tracing tools such as Langfuse, LangSmith and Phoenix give you the same view for your own application (Observability module).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How does context engineering differ from prompt engineering?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Prompt engineering is writing and organising the instructions. Context engineering covers everything the model reads on each call, including tool definitions, retrieved data, memory and history, and it happens again on every call as the context changes, so it is an ongoing runtime process rather than a one-off writing task.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give three reasons not to fill a 1M-token window just because it is available.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Quality drops as input grows (context rot and a stretched attention budget); every input token is billed on every call; and prefill time grows with input, so the time to the first token gets worse.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">An agent makes 40 tool calls per task. Why do context decisions matter more than in a single-call app?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The context is rebuilt and re-read 40 times, and each tool result stays in the history for all later calls, so one bloated result or stale instruction is paid for and attended to dozens of times.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:context/anatomy-of-a-context-window":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The context window holds \u003ci>everything\u003c/i> in one request plus the output: system prompt, tool definitions, memory, retrieved documents, the conversation history (including tool calls and results), the new message, and the tokens the model generates, including hidden reasoning. Knowing the parts and their sizes is the first step to managing them.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The parts\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Part\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it holds\u003c/th>\u003cth>Changes\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical size (tokens)\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>System prompt\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Role, rules, output format, examples\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Per release\u003c/td>\u003ctd>500 to 5,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tool definitions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Name, description and JSON schema of every tool offered\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Per release\u003c/td>\u003ctd>100 to 1,000 per tool\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Memory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>User profile, saved notes, instruction files such as AGENTS.md\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Per session\u003c/td>\u003ctd>200 to 5,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieved data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Search results, document chunks, database rows\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Per turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2,000 to 50,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>History\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Earlier messages, tool calls, tool results, kept reasoning\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Grows every turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0 to the whole window\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Scratchpad\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Plans and todo lists the agent keeps in the conversation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Per step\u003c/td>\u003ctd>100 to 2,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Current turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The new user message or latest tool result\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Every call\u003c/td>\u003ctd>50 to 5,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Output reserve\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Room for the answer and any reasoning (\u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Per call\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,000 to 64,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The sizes are illustrative ranges; measure your own.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The order on the wire\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>+---------------------------------------------+  &lt;- first token sent\n| tool definitions                            |\n| system prompt                               |\n| memory and stable reference                 |\n| history, oldest to newest                   |\n|   (messages, tool calls, tool results)      |\n| current turn: retrieved docs, the question  |\n+---------------------------------------------+\n| output reserve: answer and any reasoning    |  &lt;- generated\n+---------------------------------------------+\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Providers render all of this into one token sequence. Anthropic's API orders it tools, then system, then messages; OpenAI also renders tool definitions and developer instructions before the conversation. The order matters for caching (lesson 10) and for attention (lesson 4).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Three typical shapes\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Illustrative snapshots, not benchmarks:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Section\u003c/th>\u003cth>Support chatbot, turn 6\u003c/th>\u003cth>RAG question\u003c/th>\u003cth>Coding agent, step 30\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>System and tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2,500\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,200\u003c/td>\u003ctd>15,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Memory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>300\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieved\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>12,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0 (pulled in by tools)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>History\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>90,000, mostly tool results\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Current turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>150\u003c/td>\u003ctd>100\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Total input\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 12,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 13,300\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 110,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The coding agent shows the common pattern: history dominates, and inside history the tool results (file contents, test logs, search output) dominate. That is where lesson 6 and the Memory module focus.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Hidden and easy-to-miss tokens\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tool schemas\u003c/b> are rendered into the prompt on every call. Anthropic described a five-server MCP setup (GitHub, Slack, Sentry, Grafana, Splunk) where 58 tool definitions took about 55,000 tokens before the conversation started, and said it had seen tool definitions take 134,000 tokens before optimisation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Provider-injected text.\u003c/b> Some features add instructions for you; enabling Anthropic's memory tool, for example, adds a memory protocol to the system prompt.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reasoning.\u003c/b> Thinking tokens count toward the window and \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> in the turn they are generated. Recent Claude models keep earlier thinking blocks in later requests by default, where they count as input; OpenAI's Responses API returns encrypted reasoning items that you replay in stateless mode.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Images and PDFs\u003c/b> become tokens too, often more than a thousand per page.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Chat template overhead:\u003c/b> role markers and separators add a few tokens per message.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>The window is shared\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Input plus output (including reasoning) must fit in the window. If you send 185,000 input tokens to a 200,000-token model with \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> set to 32,000, the request is rejected or the output is cut short, depending on the provider. Always reserve the output first (lesson 3).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Measure, do not guess. Use the provider's counter (Anthropic's \u003ccode>count_tokens\u003c/code> endpoint, \u003ccode>tiktoken\u003c/code> for OpenAI models, the Hugging Face tokeniser for open-weight models) and the \u003ccode>usage\u003c/code> object returned with each response. Log token counts \u003ci>per section\u003c/i>, not just the total, so you can see which part grew. For coding agents, run the built-in context view once on your own setup (\u003ccode>/context\u003c/code> in Claude Code); most people are surprised by how much their MCP servers and instruction files take before they type anything.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">List the parts of a context window in the order they are usually sent.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Tool definitions, system prompt, memory and stable reference material, conversation history from oldest to newest (including tool calls and results), then the current turn with any retrieved data. The generated output, including reasoning, also counts toward the window.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can an agent with ten MCP servers attached perform worse before the user has typed anything?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every tool schema is rendered into the context on every call, so tens of thousands of tokens go to definitions, leaving less room and diluting attention, and many similar tools make the right choice ambiguous.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A model has a 200k window. Your input is 185k tokens and you set \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> to 32k. What happens?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Input plus requested output exceeds the window, so the API rejects the request or truncates the output, depending on the provider. You need to cut the input or lower \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>, remembering that reasoning tokens also need room.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:context-windows-and-token-budgets":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>token budget\u003c/b> splits your context into sections, each with a cap, inside a \u003ci>working\u003c/i> size that is usually far below the model's maximum. Reserve the output first, pay the fixed costs (system prompt, tools), then fill the variable sections (memory, documents, history) in priority order. When something has to give, cut the lowest-priority section, never the current user message.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Hard limit versus working budget\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The model's window (128k, 200k or 1M tokens) is a hard limit. Your \u003cb>working budget\u003c/b> is the size at which your evals show the best trade-off between quality, cost and latency. For many chat and RAG applications that is 10k to 50k tokens of input, even on a 1M-token model, because quality degrades with length (lesson 4), cost grows linearly, and time to first token grows with prefill. Choose the number from measurements, then enforce it in code.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The allocation recipe\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Output reserve.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> for the answer plus any reasoning budget.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Safety margin.\u003c/b> 2 to 5% for tokeniser mismatch and template overhead, because your local count will not match the provider's exactly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fixed costs.\u003c/b> System prompt and tool definitions. Measure them once per release.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Variable sections, by priority.\u003c/b> Current message (never cut), pinned memory, retrieved documents, conversation history.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Overflow policy.\u003c/b> When the total exceeds the working budget: drop the lowest-scoring documents, then compact or trim history, then shorten memory. Log every time this fires.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Worked example: a support assistant\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The model has a 128,000-token window. Evals showed answers stop improving above about 32k input tokens, and p95 time to first token must stay under 2 seconds.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Section\u003c/th>\u003cth>Budget\u003c/th>\u003cth>Rule\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Output reserve\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>max_tokens=4000\u003c/code>, no extended reasoning\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Safety margin\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Not allocated\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>System prompt\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,500\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fixed and versioned\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tool definitions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2,500\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Six tools, fixed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>User memory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>500\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Profile and preferences, capped\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieved documents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>12,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Highest-scoring chunks until the cap, about 8 of 1,500\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Conversation history\u003c/td>\u003ctd>14,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Newest turns first; older turns summarised\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Current user message\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,500\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Never cut; long pastes go to an attachment flow\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Working input total\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>32,000\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Plus the 4,000 output reserve: 36,000 of 128,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The other 90,000 tokens are deliberate headroom, not waste.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Enforcing it in code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import tiktoken\n\nenc = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"o200k_base\")  # an estimate for non-OpenAI models\n\ndef n_tokens(text: str) -&gt; int:\n    return len(enc.encode(text))\n\ndef fit(items: list[str], cap: int) -&gt; list[str]:\n    \"\"\"Keep items in priority order until the cap is reached.\"\"\"\n    kept, used = [], 0\n    for item in items:\n        t = n_tokens(item)\n        if used + t &gt; cap:\n            break\n        kept.append(item)\n        used += t\n    return kept\n\nBUDGET = {\"memory\": 500, \"docs\": 12_000, \"history\": 14_000}\n\ndef assemble(system, memory, docs_by_score, turns_newest_first, user_msg):\n    return {\n        \"system\": system,\n        \"memory\": fit(memory, BUDGET[\"memory\"]),\n        \"docs\": fit(docs_by_score, BUDGET[\"docs\"]),\n        \"history\": list(reversed(fit(turns_newest_first, BUDGET[\"history\"]))),\n        \"user\": user_msg,  # never cut\n    }\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Real implementations also keep each tool call together with its result, and summarise what falls out of the history budget instead of silently dropping it (Memory module). You will build a full version in this module's Atlas milestone.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Count with the right tokeniser.\u003c/b> Model families split the same text differently. Use the provider's counting endpoint or the \u003ccode>usage\u003c/code> it returns for exact numbers, and treat local counts as estimates.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Watch real usage.\u003c/b> Compare input tokens (including cached ones) against the budget on every request and alert at, say, 80%.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Budget reasoning separately.\u003c/b> Reasoning models can spend thousands of output tokens before answering; give them their own reserve and an effort level.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Degrade, do not crash.\u003c/b> An overflow should shrink sections, not show the user an error.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Budget tokens for the system prompt, history, documents and output of a support assistant on a 128k-token model.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Start from a working budget set by evals, for example 32k input plus a 4k output reserve and a 2k safety margin. Inside the 32k: system prompt 1.5k, tool definitions 2.5k, user memory 0.5k, retrieved documents 12k (highest-scoring chunks until the cap), history 14k (newest turns first, older ones summarised) and up to 1.5k for the current message, which is never cut. The remaining 90k of the window is headroom; filling it would raise cost and latency and usually lower quality.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why reserve the output before allocating input?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Input and output share the window. If the input fills it, the model has no room to answer or reason, and the request fails or the reply is truncated.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your history section keeps hitting its cap. Name two responses other than raising the cap.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Summarise or compact older turns into a short summary; clear old tool results that have already been used; or move durable facts into memory so they no longer need to live in the history.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:position-effects-lost-in-the-middle":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Models do not use long inputs uniformly. Accuracy falls as input grows, even on simple tasks (\u003cb>context rot\u003c/b>); facts in the middle of a long input are used worse than facts at the start or end (\u003cb>lost in the middle\u003c/b>); and plausible but wrong passages (\u003cb>distractors\u003c/b>) cause errors that grow with length. Shorter, cleaner context and careful placement beat a bigger window.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lost in the middle (2023)\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Liu and colleagues gave models a question plus 10, 20 or 30 retrieved documents, exactly one of which contained the answer, and moved that document from first to last position. Accuracy traced a \u003cb>U shape\u003c/b>: highest when the answer was at the very start (primacy) or the very end (recency), lowest in the middle. For GPT-3.5-Turbo with 20 documents, accuracy with the answer in the middle fell below its \u003ci>closed-book\u003c/i> accuracy of 56.1%: the documents made it worse than having none at all. Extended-context versions of the same models were no better at using their context.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Context rot (2025)\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Chroma's July 2025 study tested 18 models, including GPT-4.1, Claude 4, Gemini 2.5 and Qwen3. It held task difficulty constant and varied only input length. Four findings matter for engineers:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Needle-in-a-haystack scores mislead.\u003c/b> The classic test hides a sentence that shares words with the question. When the needle matched the question only by meaning, performance fell much faster as input grew.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Distractors hurt, more so at length.\u003c/b> Even one topically similar but wrong passage lowered accuracy, four compounded the drop, and some distractors did far more damage than others.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Structure matters in surprising ways.\u003c/b> Across all 18 models, needles were found more easily in \u003ci>shuffled\u003c/i> haystacks than in coherent essays.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Focused beats full.\u003c/b> On LongMemEval conversations, every model did much better with a focused input (about 300 tokens of relevant history) than with the full input (about 113,000 tokens). Thinking modes helped but did not close the gap.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Newer models degrade more gently, but Anthropic notes the effect emerges across all models. Treat it as a property of current LLMs, not a bug in one release.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What to do about it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Finding\u003c/th>\u003cth>Practice\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Length alone hurts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Keep a working budget well under the maximum (lesson 3); compact history (Memory module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Start and end are used best\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Long documents first, question and key instructions last; Anthropic reports that queries at the end improved response quality by up to 30% in its tests with complex multi-document inputs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>The middle is weakest\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Do not bury critical constraints mid-prompt; restate them near the end; keep a short task list that is rewritten at the end of the context (Manus calls this recitation)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Distractors hurt\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rerank and cut retrieved passages to the few that answer; remove near-duplicates and outdated versions\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Meaning-only matches degrade fastest\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Write queries and instructions in the vocabulary of the source; quote exact identifiers and names\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Placement and caching agree more than it seems: long \u003ci>stable\u003c/i> documents (a contract, a handbook) go early, which also suits caching; per-turn retrieved passages go at the start of the current user turn; the question goes last.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Test it on your model\u003c/h4>\u003cp>This is the classic experiment. Build long inputs, hide one fact at different depths, ask for it, and count hits:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\n\ndef make_haystack(filler: list[str], fact: str, depth: float, n_tokens: int) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Insert fact at a relative depth (0.0 = start, 1.0 = end) of about n_tokens of filler.\"\"\"\n    paras, size = [], 0\n    while size &lt; n_tokens:\n        p = random.choice(filler)\n        paras.append(p)\n        size += len(p) // 4  # rough estimate: 4 characters per token\n    paras.insert(int(len(paras) * depth), fact)\n    return \"\\n\\n\".join(paras)\n\nFACT = \"The access code for the Lisbon warehouse is 7319.\"\nQUESTION = \"What is the access code for the Lisbon warehouse? Answer with the number only.\"\n\nfor size in (8_000, 32_000, 128_000):\n    for depth in (0.1, 0.5, 0.9):\n        hits = sum(\"7319\" in ask(make_haystack(FILLER, FACT, depth, size) + \"\\n\\n\" + QUESTION)\n                   for _ in range(20))  # ask() is your model call\n        print(f\"{size:&gt;7} tokens, depth {depth}: {hits / 20:.0%}\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Use realistic filler (your own documents), then run two harder variants: add a distractor such as \"The access code for the Lisbon office is 4420\", and paraphrase the question (\"How do I get into our storage site in Portugal?\") so it no longer shares words with the fact.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Concluding \"our model has no long-context problem\" from one lexical needle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Fixing a mid-prompt failure by adding more text, which pushes the instruction further into the middle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Assuming results transfer between models; the curve differs, so test the model you ship.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Place a key fact at three positions in a long context and measure recall: how would you run it, and what do you expect?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Build inputs from realistic filler at several sizes (for example 8k, 32k and 128k tokens), insert one fact at about 10%, 50% and 90% depth, ask a question whose answer is that fact, and run 20 or more trials per cell, scoring exact matches. Expect recall near 100% at short lengths, falling as length grows, with the middle position usually lowest (a U shape); adding a similar distractor or paraphrasing the question widens the drop. Newer models flatten the curve but rarely remove it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is the classic needle-in-a-haystack test a weak measure of long-context ability?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The needle usually shares exact words with the question, so it tests lexical lookup. Real tasks need matching by meaning, ignoring distractors and combining several facts, and those degrade much more with length.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Where do you put retrieved passages, the question and the key instructions?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Passages at the start of the current user turn (stable long documents even earlier), and the question plus the most important instructions at the end, closest to where the model starts generating.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:context-for-tools-just-in-time-retrieval":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Preloading\u003c/b> puts everything that might help into the prompt before the model starts. \u003cb>Just-in-time (JIT)\u003c/b> context gives the model lightweight references (file paths, document ids, URLs, saved queries) plus tools to load content only when it decides it needs it. JIT keeps context small and fresh at the cost of extra round trips; most strong agents mix both.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Three ways to get knowledge in\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Approach\u003c/th>\u003cth>How\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tokens per request\u003c/th>\u003cth>Freshness\u003c/th>\u003cth>Main failure\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Preload\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Paste all relevant documents into the prompt\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Large and fixed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>As fresh as the last paste\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Context rot, cost\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Pre-inference retrieval (classic RAG)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your code searches and inserts the top chunks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Depends on the index\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Wrong chunks; the model cannot ask again\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Just-in-time (agentic search)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The model calls search and read tools as it works\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Small; grows only as needed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Live\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The model does not search, or searches too much\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>References, not payloads\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The core move is to put a \u003ci>pointer\u003c/i> in context instead of the content. A pointer is cheap and still carries signal: a file called \u003ccode>tests/test_billing.py\u003c/code> tells the model what it holds, sizes hint at complexity and dates hint at relevance. The model then pulls exactly the part it needs:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Available documents (call read_doc(id, section) to open one):\n- refunds-policy (2.4k tokens, updated 2026-09-30): refunds, exceptions, chargebacks\n- shipping-eu (1.8k tokens, updated 2026-10-12): EU delivery times, customs\n- warranty (3.1k tokens, updated 2026-03-02): coverage, claims, exclusions\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Manus applies the same idea to compression: a web page's content can be dropped from context as long as its URL stays, and a file's contents as long as its path stays. The information is \u003ci>restorable\u003c/i>, so nothing is lost for good.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Progressive disclosure\u003c/h4>\u003cp>JIT works best in layers: names and one-line descriptions first, then headings or a summary, then the full text of one section. Agent Skills (folders with a \u003ccode>SKILL.md\u003c/code> file) are built this way: only each skill's name and description sit in context until the model activates one, and only then is its body loaded. Claude Code mixes both styles: instruction files are loaded up front, while code is found with \u003ccode>glob\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>grep\u003c/code> when needed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked example: 40 policy documents\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Preloaded:\u003c/b> 40 documents of about 2,500 tokens each, plus 1,000 tokens of system prompt and question, is about \u003cb>101,000 input tokens\u003c/b> on every request.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>JIT\u003c/b> with two tools (\u003ccode>search_docs\u003c/code> returns 5 titles and snippets, about 400 tokens; \u003ccode>read_doc\u003c/code> returns one section, about 2,500 tokens):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Call\u003c/th>\u003cth>Input tokens\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1: system prompt, tool definitions (350), question\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,350\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2: the above plus the tool call and search results\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,800\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>3: the above plus the tool call and one section\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4,350\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Total for the question\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>7,500\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>That is about 13 times fewer input tokens, and the answer comes from a fresh, focused section. The price is three model calls instead of one, but each call prefills far fewer tokens, so end-to-end latency is often similar.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The tools\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>TOOLS = [\n    {\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n        \"name\": \"search_docs\",\n        \"description\": \"Search the policy docs. Returns up to k results with id, title, section \"\n                       \"and a one-line snippet. Use before answering any policy question.\",\n        \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\n            \"query\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n            \"k\": {\"type\": \"integer\", \"default\": 5}},\n            \"required\": [\"query\"]}}},\n    {\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n        \"name\": \"read_doc\",\n        \"description\": \"Read one section of a doc by id. Omit section to get its table of contents.\",\n        \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\n            \"id\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n            \"section\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n            \"required\": [\"id\"]}}},\n]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>When to preload anyway\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Preload content that is \u003cb>small, stable and needed on almost every call\u003c/b>: a style guide, core policies, a glossary, the user's profile. It is cheap, avoids a round trip and, because it does not change, caches well (lesson 10). Use JIT for the rest: large, changing or rarely needed material.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>The model never searches.\u003c/b> Say when to search in the system prompt and in the tool description (\"Use before answering any policy question\").\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Search loops.\u003c/b> Cap tool calls per turn, and return \"no results; try different terms\" rather than an empty list.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Uninformative references.\u003c/b> Opaque ids such as \u003ccode>a7f3c9\u003c/code> give no signal; use names, titles, sizes and dates.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Stale indexes.\u003c/b> Pointers are only as fresh as the index behind them.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Replace preloaded documents with a search tool and compare tokens: what changes, and what do you measure?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Remove the pasted documents, give the model a manifest or a \u003ccode>search_docs\u003c/code> tool plus a \u003ccode>read_doc\u003c/code> tool, and tell it to search before answering. Measure total input tokens per question across all calls, the number of calls, end-to-end latency and answer accuracy on the same question set. Input tokens typically fall by an order of magnitude (for example from 101k to 7.5k for 40 documents) with equal or better accuracy, at the cost of two or three extra model calls.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What makes a good reference in a JIT design?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is cheap and informative: a meaningful name or path, a one-line description, size and date, so the model can decide whether to open it without loading it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When is preloading the better choice?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When the material is small, stable and needed on nearly every request, such as core rules, a glossary or a user profile; it saves round trips and caches well.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:context/tool-output-hygiene":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every tool result is appended to the history and re-read on every later call in the loop. One careless tool that returns a whole log, page or API payload can consume the budget and bury the task. Filter, paginate and truncate by default, return human-meaningful fields, and write error messages that tell the model what to do next.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why it compounds\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An agent runs 20 steps. At step 3 a \u003ccode>get_logs\u003c/code> tool returns 30,000 tokens. That result is re-sent on steps 4 to 20: 17 × 30,000 = \u003cb>510,000 extra input tokens\u003c/b>. Prompt caching cuts the price of re-reading it, but not the attention cost: the model still has to look past 30,000 tokens of logs on every step. Capped at 2,000 tokens with a pointer to the full output, the same tool adds 34,000.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Techniques\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Technique\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it looks like\u003c/th>\u003cth>Effect\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Filter at the source\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>search_orders(customer_id, status=\"open\")\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>list_orders()\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only relevant rows enter context\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Paginate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>limit=20\u003c/code> and a \u003ccode>next_cursor\u003c/code> in the result\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The model asks for more only if needed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Truncate with guidance\u003c/td>\u003ctd>First 2,000 tokens plus \"Truncated: 48,210 more lines. Use grep_logs(pattern) to narrow.\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bounded size, and the model learns a better strategy\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Range selection\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>read_file(path, start_line, end_line)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Opens only the part needed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Response format switch\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>response_format=\"concise\"\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>\"detailed\"\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Anthropic's Slack example: 72 tokens concise against 206 detailed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Meaningful fields\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Keep \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>title\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>status\u003c/code>; drop \u003ccode>uuid\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>mime_type\u003c/code>, internal URLs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fewer tokens and fewer invented ids\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Store and reference\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Write big output to a file; return the path and a 20-line head\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Full data stays available, context stays small\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Summarise\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A small, cheap model condenses a long page before it is returned\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cheaper than the main model re-reading it every step\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Anthropic reports that resolving arbitrary alphanumeric UUIDs to meaningful names, or even a simple 0-based index, significantly improved retrieval precision by reducing hallucinated identifiers. When a later call needs the raw id, return both.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A wrapper every tool can use\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json, pathlib, uuid\n\nMAX_TOKENS = 2_000  # cap per tool result\n\ndef tool_result(data, hint: str = \"\", spill_dir: str = \"tool_outputs\") -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Serialise a tool result; spill oversized results to a file and return a pointer.\"\"\"\n    text = data if isinstance(data, str) else json.dumps(data, sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False)\n    approx_tokens = len(text) // 4  # rough estimate: 4 characters per token\n    if approx_tokens &lt;= MAX_TOKENS:\n        return text\n    path = pathlib.Path(spill_dir) / f\"{uuid.uuid4().hex[:8]}.txt\"\n    path.parent.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    path.write_text(text)\n    head = text[: MAX_TOKENS * 4]\n    return (f\"{head}\\n\\n[Truncated: about {approx_tokens:,} tokens in total, saved to {path}. \"\n            f\"{hint or 'Use read_file(path, start_line, end_line) or a narrower query.'}]\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>sort_keys=True\u003c/code> matters: identical data then always produces identical tokens, which keeps prompt caches valid (lesson 10).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Errors that guide\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An unhelpful error wastes a step:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Error 400\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A helpful one teaches the fix:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Error: 'since' must be an ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD), got \"last Tuesday\".\nExample: search_orders(customer_id=\"C-1042\", since=\"2026-10-21\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Return errors as ordinary tool results (with the API's error flag if it has one) so the model can correct itself, instead of raising an exception that ends the loop.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Claude Code caps tool responses at 25,000 tokens by default. Choose your own cap per tool from what you see in traces.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Log token counts per tool call and look at the distribution: one or two tools usually account for most of the context.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Once a result has been used, it can later be cleared from history entirely (context editing, Memory module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Treat tool output as untrusted: a web page or email can contain instructions aimed at the model (Security module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A tool returns 30,000 tokens at step 3 of a 20-step agent run. How many extra input tokens does that add, and what are two fixes?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is re-sent on steps 4 to 20, so about 17 × 30,000 = 510,000 input tokens. Fixes: cap and truncate the result with a hint to narrow the query, paginate or filter at the source, or spill the full output to a file and return the path and a short head; later, clear the result from history once it has been used.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why return names instead of UUIDs where possible?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Names are meaningful to the model, cost fewer tokens and reduce invented or mistyped identifiers; Anthropic found that resolving UUIDs to readable names improved retrieval precision. Include the raw id only when a later call needs it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What should a good tool error message contain?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>What was wrong, what valid input looks like and ideally an example call, so the model can fix its call on the next step instead of guessing.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:instruction-files-agents-md":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Instruction files are persistent, version-controlled context that coding agents load at the start of every session: how to build and test, conventions, layout and known gotchas. \u003cb>AGENTS.md\u003c/b> is the open, cross-tool format; Claude Code uses \u003cb>CLAUDE.md\u003c/b> and reads AGENTS.md when no CLAUDE.md exists; several tools add path-scoped rule files. Because they load every session, every line costs tokens and attention, so keep them short and concrete.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The landscape (late 2026)\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>File\u003c/th>\u003cth>Read by\u003c/th>\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>AGENTS.md\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI, the GitHub Copilot coding agent, Jules, Aider, Zed, Devin and many more\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Open Markdown format used by over 60,000 open-source projects; stewarded by the Agentic AI Foundation under the Linux Foundation\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>CLAUDE.md\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Claude Code\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Organisation, user (\u003ccode>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md\u003c/code>), project and local scopes; reads \u003ccode>AGENTS.md\u003c/code> instead when there is no \u003ccode>CLAUDE.md\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Path-scoped rules\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Claude Code (\u003ccode>.claude/rules/\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>paths:\u003c/code>), GitHub Copilot (\u003ccode>.github/instructions/*.instructions.md\u003c/code>), Cursor (\u003ccode>.cursor/rules/\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Load only when the agent works on matching files\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Skills\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Many agents (the Agent Skills format)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Procedures loaded on demand, not in every session\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How they load\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Hierarchy.\u003c/b> Agents read a global file, then files from the repository root down to the current directory. For AGENTS.md, the file closest to the edited file wins, and an explicit instruction from the user in chat overrides everything. Codex concatenates files from the root down, so later (closer) files override earlier guidance, and stops adding files at 32 KiB by default. Claude Code loads files above the working directory at launch and files in subdirectories on demand.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Monorepos.\u003c/b> A short root file plus one per package. When agents.md was written, OpenAI's main repository had 88 AGENTS.md files.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Compaction.\u003c/b> In Claude Code the project-root CLAUDE.md is re-read from disk after \u003ccode>/compact\u003c/code>, so rules there survive a summary. Instructions you only typed in chat do not.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>What to put in, and what to leave out\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Put in\u003c/th>\u003cth>Leave out\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Exact build, test, lint and run commands\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Anything the agent can read from the code in seconds\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Conventions that differ from defaults (\"use uv\", \"no ORM in handlers\")\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Long tutorials and architecture essays\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Where things live (\"API handlers in \u003ccode>src/api/handlers/\u003c/code>\")\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Secrets, tokens, URLs with credentials\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Gotchas that caused real mistakes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Vague advice (\"write clean code\")\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>How to verify work before finishing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rules for one folder (use a path-scoped file)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Commit and pull request conventions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Multi-step procedures (use a skill)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>An example AGENTS.md\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">markdown\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode># AGENTS.md\n\n## Project\nAtlas: FastAPI service and CLI that answers questions over our docs.\nPython 3.12, uv, Postgres 16 with pgvector. Entry point: atlas/app.py.\n\n## Commands\n- Install: `uv sync`\n- Tests: `uv run pytest -q` (start the database first: `docker compose up -d db`)\n- Lint and types: `uv run ruff check . &amp;&amp; uv run mypy atlas`\n- Run locally: `uv run atlas serve --reload`\n\n## Conventions\n- All model calls go through atlas/llm.py; never import a provider SDK elsewhere.\n- Prompts live in prompts/*.md with a version header; never inline prompts in code.\n- Every tool input and output is a Pydantic model in atlas/tools/schemas.py.\n- Log with structlog; never print.\n\n## Gotchas\n- Tests that call a real model are marked @pytest.mark.live and skipped in CI.\n- eval/data/ is generated; do not edit it by hand, run `uv run atlas eval build`.\n\n## Before you finish\n- Lint, types and tests must all pass.\n- Update CHANGELOG.md for user-visible changes.\n- Commit messages: \"&lt;area&gt;: &lt;imperative summary&gt;\", e.g. \"retrieval: add BM25 fallback\".\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>About 30 lines: enough to prevent the common mistakes, small enough to cost little in every session.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Keep it short.\u003c/b> Claude Code's docs suggest under 200 lines per file, because longer files cost more context and reduce adherence.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Write rules you could check.\u003c/b> \"Run \u003ccode>uv run pytest -q\u003c/code> before committing\" beats \"test your changes\"; \"2-space indentation\" beats \"format code properly\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Add a line when the agent makes the same mistake twice\u003c/b>, or when a reviewer catches something it should have known.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Avoid contradictions\u003c/b> between nested files and rules; the model may pick one arbitrarily.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Instructions are context, not enforcement.\u003c/b> Use CI checks, hooks and permissions for rules that must never be broken.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>One source of truth.\u003c/b> If you use several tools, keep AGENTS.md as the source and import it (Claude Code supports an \u003ccode>@AGENTS.md\u003c/code> import) instead of maintaining copies.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Review changes to instruction files in pull requests, like code.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Write an AGENTS.md for one of your repositories: what sections and content should it contain?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A short project line (stack and entry point); exact commands to install, test, lint and run; conventions that differ from defaults (for example where model calls, prompts, schemas and logging live); gotchas that caused real mistakes; and a \"before you finish\" checklist (lint, types and tests pass, changelog, commit format), as in the example above. Keep it under about 200 lines, concrete and checkable, with no secrets and nothing the agent can read from the code, and move package-specific rules into nested files or path-scoped rules.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">The root AGENTS.md says \"use npm\" and \u003ccode>packages/web/AGENTS.md\u003c/code> says \"use pnpm\". Which applies when the agent edits a file in \u003ccode>packages/web\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The closest file to the edited file, so pnpm. An explicit instruction from the user in the chat overrides both.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why not put everything you know about the project into the instruction file?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It loads into every session, so every line costs tokens and attention, and long files reduce adherence. Keep always-needed facts there, and move procedures into skills and folder-specific rules into path-scoped files that load only when needed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:context/subagents-for-context-isolation":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>subagent\u003c/b> is a separate model loop with its own clean context window. The parent sends a focused brief; the subagent does the token-heavy work (searching, reading files, scanning logs) and returns only a condensed result, often 1,000 to 2,000 tokens. The parent's context stays small and on task, at the price of more total tokens.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The mechanism\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Parent\u003c/b>\u003csmall>needs facts\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Brief\u003c/b>\u003csmall>goal, scope, format\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Subagent\u003c/b>\u003csmall>own window, reads 80k tokens\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Result\u003c/b>\u003csmall>1.5k-token summary\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Parent\u003c/b>\u003csmall>continues, context still small\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Without a subagent the parent would read all 80,000 tokens itself, and they would sit in its history for the rest of the task. With one, the parent's history grows only by the brief and the summary. Anthropic describes search as compression: subagents explore in parallel, each in its own window, and condense the most important tokens for the lead agent.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A minimal implementation\u003c/h4>\u003cp>To the parent, a subagent is just another tool:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def run_agent(system: str, user: str, tools: list, max_steps: int = 20) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"A plain tool loop (see the Tools module); returns the final text.\"\"\"\n    ...\n\ndef delegate(task: str, return_format: str) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Tool offered to the parent agent.\"\"\"\n    brief = (\n        f\"Task: {task}\\n\"\n        f\"Return format: {return_format}\\n\"\n        \"Return at most 300 words. Cite a file path or URL for every claim. \"\n        \"List open questions separately. Never paste raw file contents.\"\n    )\n    return run_agent(SUBAGENT_SYSTEM, brief, tools=READ_ONLY_TOOLS, max_steps=15)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The parent's tool description says when to use it, for example: \"Use delegate for any investigation that needs more than three files read.\"\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What goes down and what comes back\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Send down (the brief)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Bring back (the result)\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>One clear objective\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Findings that answer the objective\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Scope and boundaries (\"only \u003ccode>src/billing\u003c/code>\", \"read-only\")\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Evidence pointers: paths, line numbers, URLs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Output format and length limit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Confidence, and what was not checked\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tools and budget (steps, tokens)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Open questions for the parent\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Context the subagent cannot find by itself\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Nothing raw: no full files, no logs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A vague brief is the commonest failure: subagents duplicate each other's work or solve a different problem. Anthropic reported that early versions of its research system spawned 50 subagents for simple queries, and that short briefs such as \"research the semiconductor shortage\" left subagents without a division of labour: one explored the 2021 automotive chip crisis while two others duplicated each other's searches on 2025 supply chains. Its fix was to give every subagent an objective, an output format, guidance on tools and sources, and clear task boundaries.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Isolation is not free. In Anthropic's data, agents used about 4 times as many tokens as chat interactions, and multi-agent systems about 15 times as many. Its multi-agent research setup (a Claude Opus 4 lead with Claude Sonnet 4 subagents) outperformed a single Opus 4 agent by 90.2% on an internal research eval, mostly on breadth-first questions that split into independent directions. Tasks with many dependencies, such as most coding, gain less, because subagents cannot easily share evolving state.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use subagents\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Good fit\u003c/th>\u003cth>Poor fit\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Broad research with independent sub-questions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tightly coupled edits across one codebase\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reading many files or long logs to answer one question\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tasks where every step depends on the previous one\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Work needing different tools or permissions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Small tasks one call can handle\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Keeping a long-running main session clean\u003c/td>\u003ctd>When total token cost is the binding constraint\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Coding agents ship this pattern. Claude Code sends research to subagents so large file reads stay in their windows and only a summary returns to yours, and you can define your own subagents with their own prompts and tools. The agent SDKs from OpenAI, Anthropic and Google offer similar agent-as-tool or handoff primitives. Keep subagents read-only where you can, give each a step and token budget, and log their full transcripts, because the parent (and your traces of it) only sees the summary. Orchestration patterns are covered in the Multi-agent module.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the context-engineering benefit of a subagent, and what does it cost?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The heavy exploration happens in a separate window, so the parent receives only a short distilled result and its context stays small and focused. The cost is more total tokens (Anthropic saw multi-agent systems use about 15 times the tokens of chat), more latency, and a risk of losing detail or provenance in the summary.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A subagent returns 20,000 tokens of copied file contents. What went wrong, and how do you fix it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The brief did not constrain the output, so the isolation was wasted. Specify the return format and a length limit, ask for findings with file paths and line numbers instead of raw contents, and validate or truncate the result before it reaches the parent.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name one task that suits subagents and one that does not.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Suits: researching a question across many sources, or reading dozens of files to locate a bug. Does not suit: a sequence of dependent edits to the same module, where every step needs the full, evolving state.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:long-context-versus-retrieval":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>With context windows of up to a million tokens you can sometimes skip retrieval and give the model the whole corpus. That wins when the material is small enough, stable and needs whole-document reading. Retrieval wins when the corpus is large or changes often, when query volume is high, and when you need precise, citable answers. Many production systems combine both.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The decision factors\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Factor\u003c/th>\u003cth>Favours long context\u003c/th>\u003cth>Favours retrieval\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Corpus size\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fits comfortably within the working budget\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bigger than the window, or far bigger than the working budget\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Change rate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Static: a contract, a book, one codebase snapshot\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Updated daily\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Query volume\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Low: a few analyses\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High: thousands of queries a day\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Task type\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Whole-document reasoning: summarise, compare sections, find contradictions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Lookup: answer from one or two passages\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Facts per query\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Many, spread across the text\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One or two\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Access control\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One user owns all the content\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Users may see different documents\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Citations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Nice to have\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Required for every claim\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked cost example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A 400,000-token handbook, an illustrative input price of $3 per million tokens, cache reads at 0.1x and cache writes at 1.25x the input price (see the Billing module for real prices and rules):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Approach\u003c/th>\u003cth>Input tokens per query\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost per query\u003c/th>\u003cth>At 10,000 queries a day\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Long context, no cache\u003c/td>\u003ctd>400,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$1.20\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$12,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Long context, cached prefix\u003c/td>\u003ctd>400,000 read from cache\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about $0.12\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about $1,200, plus a $1.50 write whenever the cache expires\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieval, 8 chunks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.024\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$240\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Latency has the same shape: a cold 400k-token prefill takes many seconds before the first token, and caching cuts it substantially. Some providers also charge a higher rate above a long-context threshold, which widens the gap.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Quality is not automatic either\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Google's long-context guide notes that its models reach about 99% accuracy finding a single \"needle\", but that accuracy drops, and varies widely, when several pieces of information must be found. Context rot (lesson 4) applies: a long context full of near-duplicates and outdated versions is a distractor-rich environment. Retrieval has the opposite failure: if the right chunk is not retrieved, the model never sees it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Hybrid patterns\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Retrieve, then read long.\u003c/b> Retrieve whole documents or large sections instead of tiny chunks, then let the model read them in a long context. This fixes answers that were split across chunks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Long context for the working set, retrieval for the library.\u003c/b> Keep the document the user is working on fully in context; search everything else.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cache a stable corpus.\u003c/b> For a fixed handbook queried all day, put it in a cached prefix and append each question after it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Agentic search\u003c/b> (lesson 5) for large structured collections such as codebases.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start with the simplest design that meets your quality bar and budget. A long-context prototype is a good first step: it shows what good answers look like when nothing is missing, giving you an upper bound and reference answers. Then measure whether retrieval keeps that quality at a fraction of the cost. Re-run the comparison when prices or models change, because the crossover point moves.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Decide between long context and RAG for three scenarios: reviewing one 150-page contract, a support bot over 20,000 help articles updated daily, and Q&amp;A over a 3-million-token codebase.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Contract: long context. At roughly 75k to 100k tokens it fits, it is static and low volume, and the task needs whole-document reasoning across cross-references. Support bot: retrieval, ideally hybrid search with reranking, because the corpus is far larger than any working budget, changes daily, query volume is high and answers need citations. Codebase: agentic just-in-time search (grep, file reads, symbol lookup) plus a short preloaded overview such as AGENTS.md, because 3M tokens exceeds the window and the code changes with every commit.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A 400k-token handbook is queried 10,000 times a day. Roughly how do uncached long context, cached long context and retrieval compare at $3 per million input tokens?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Uncached: 0.4M × $3 = $1.20 per query, about $12,000 a day. Cached reads at 0.1x: about $0.12 per query, about $1,200 a day plus cache writes. Retrieval with 8k tokens: $0.024 per query, about $240 a day.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is a long-context prototype useful even if you plan to ship RAG?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It shows what the model can do when all the information is present, without retrieval errors, so it gives an upper bound and reference answers to compare the RAG system against.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:context/cache-aware-context-layout":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Prompt caches reuse the model's work for an \u003cb>exact prefix\u003c/b> of your request; one changed token invalidates everything after it. So lay out context from most stable to most volatile, keep history append-only, serialise deterministically, and put timestamps, per-user data and retrieved documents late. Prices and provider rules are in the Billing module; this lesson is about layout.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The ladder\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Tools\u003c/b>\u003csmall>fixed, sorted\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>System prompt\u003c/b>\u003csmall>versioned\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Stable reference\u003c/b>\u003csmall>glossary, policies\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Session memory\u003c/b>\u003csmall>user profile\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>History\u003c/b>\u003csmall>append-only\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Current turn\u003c/b>\u003csmall>docs, date, question\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each rung changes more often than the one before it. A cache hit covers everything up to the first rung that changed. Manus, whose agent averages about 100 input tokens per output token, calls the KV-cache hit rate the single most important metric for a production-stage agent, because it drives both latency and cost.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Where dynamic data goes\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Item\u003c/th>\u003cth>Wrong place\u003c/th>\u003cth>Right place\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Current date and time\u003c/td>\u003ctd>First line of the system prompt, to the second\u003c/td>\u003ctd>In the current user turn, at the granularity the task needs (usually the date)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>User name and preferences\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Interpolated into the system prompt text\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A memory block after the stable system prompt, or the first user message\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieved passages\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Appended to the system prompt\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Inside the current user turn, before the question\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Available tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Added or removed per step\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A fixed list; restrict with \u003ccode>tool_choice\u003c/code>, an allowed-tools option, or masking\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Feature flags and A/B variants\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Edits in the middle of the prompt\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A separate stable prefix per variant\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Request and trace ids\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Anywhere in the prompt\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Headers and logs only\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Manus found that adding or removing tools mid-task both breaks the cache (tool definitions sit near the front) and confuses the model when earlier history refers to tools that have disappeared; it keeps the list fixed and masks choices instead. OpenAI's guide gives the same advice: keep tool definitions stable, use \u003ccode>tool_choice: \"none\"\u003c/code> or an allowed-tools list instead of removing tools, and when tools are discovered later, append them at the end.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Silent cache breakers\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A timestamp, request id or random value near the top.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Non-deterministic serialisation, such as \u003ccode>json.dumps(obj)\u003c/code> on a dict built in varying order. Use \u003ccode>sort_keys=True\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Rewriting earlier messages: editing a past turn, re-summarising history every turn, or trimming one message from the front each turn.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Changing settings that are rendered into the prefix: on many providers the model, the tool list, the structured-output schema and the reasoning effort or thinking settings.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Images or documents re-encoded differently on each request.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Provider mechanics in brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>As of late 2026 all major providers cache prefixes, automatically or with light markup. Anthropic caches in the order tools, system, messages, up to \u003ccode>cache_control\u003c/code> breakpoints you place (up to four) or an automatic breakpoint that advances as the conversation grows; the default lifetime is 5 minutes, refreshed free on every hit. OpenAI caches automatically from 1,024 tokens on recent models, supports explicit breakpoints on GPT-5.6 and later, and lists the settings that break the prefix (model, tools, \u003ccode>parallel_tool_calls\u003c/code>, output format, reasoning effort, verbosity and compaction). Gemini uses implicit caching. Every provider reports cached tokens in \u003ccode>usage\u003c/code>, so you can compute a \u003cb>hit rate\u003c/b>: cached input tokens divided by all input tokens.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Putting it together\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def build_request(stable, session, turn):\n    \"\"\"Anthropic-style request, stable to volatile: one explicit breakpoint plus automatic caching.\"\"\"\n    system = [\n        {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": stable.system_prompt},\n        {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": stable.reference,\n         \"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}},       # shared by every user\n        {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": session.profile_text},  # per user, stable within a session\n    ]\n    content = []\n    if turn.docs:\n        content.append({\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": render_docs(turn.docs)})\n    content.append({\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": f\"Today is {turn.date:%Y-%m-%d}.\\n\\n{turn.question}\"})\n    return {\n        \"model\": MODEL,\n        \"max_tokens\": 2000,\n        \"tools\": stable.tools,                          # fixed, sorted by name\n        \"system\": system,\n        \"messages\": session.history + [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": content}],  # append-only\n        \"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"},         # automatic breakpoint at the end\n    }\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The explicit breakpoint keeps the shared prefix cached across users; the automatic one caches each conversation as it grows, so turn n reads everything up to turn n-1 from cache.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Track the \u003cb>hit rate per route\u003c/b> and alert when it drops; a sudden drop almost always means someone added a dynamic value to the prefix.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Judge by \u003cb>total cost\u003c/b>, not hit rate alone: compaction lowers the hit rate on one request but can cut total input a lot (Memory module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Mind the \u003cb>lifetime\u003c/b>: after a long pause the cache is cold anyway, which is a good moment for any change that would break it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Self-hosting: enable prefix caching in vLLM or SGLang and route a session's requests to the same replica, because a cache on another machine does not help.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your system prompt starts with \"Current time: 14:32:07\". What happens to caching, and how do you fix it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The first tokens change on every request, so no prefix ever matches and every call pays full price for the whole prompt. Move the time into the current user turn at the granularity the task needs (often just the date), and keep the system prompt byte-identical across requests.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">An agent loads a different set of tools at each step to save tokens. What are the two problems?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Tool definitions sit near the front of the prompt, so any change invalidates the cache for everything after them; and earlier history may refer to tools that no longer exist, confusing the model. Keep the list fixed and restrict choices with tool_choice, an allowed-tools option or masking, or use tool search that appends new tools at the end.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you compute the cache hit rate from usage data?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Divide cached input tokens (cache reads) by all input tokens (uncached input plus cache reads plus cache writes), per request or summed over a period.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:context/context-budget-analyser":"\u003cp>You will capture one real request from an agent or assistant, split it into sections and count each one. Most people find one section they did not expect taking a third of the window.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.11+ and \u003ccode>pip install tiktoken\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>One captured request body as JSON, taken late in a session (step 15 or later of an agent run, or turn 10 or later of a chat). Ways to get one: log it from your Atlas client (M1 logs requests), run any agent through a logging proxy such as LiteLLM, or export a trace from Langfuse or LangSmith.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save the request as \u003ccode>request.json\u003c/code>. It should contain \u003ccode>tools\u003c/code>, the system prompt (a \u003ccode>system\u003c/code> field or a system or developer message) and \u003ccode>messages\u003c/code>. Both the OpenAI-style and the Anthropic-style shapes work with the script below.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Save this as \u003ccode>analyse.py\u003c/code>:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import collections, json, sys\nimport tiktoken\n\nenc = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"o200k_base\")\n\ndef tok(x) -&gt; int:\n    s = x if isinstance(x, str) else json.dumps(x, sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False)\n    return len(enc.encode(s))\n\nreq = json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))\nparts, biggest = collections.Counter(), []\nparts[\"tools\"] = tok(req.get(\"tools\", []))\nparts[\"system\"] = tok(req.get(\"system\", \"\"))\n\nfor m in req.get(\"messages\", []):\n    role, content = m.get(\"role\"), m.get(\"content\")\n    blocks = content if isinstance(content, list) else [{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": content or \"\"}]\n    for b in blocks:\n        kind = b.get(\"type\", \"text\") if isinstance(b, dict) else \"text\"\n        if role in (\"system\", \"developer\"):\n            key = \"system\"\n        elif role == \"tool\" or kind == \"tool_result\":\n            key = \"tool results\"\n            biggest.append((tok(b), json.dumps(b)[:80]))\n        elif kind == \"tool_use\":\n            key = \"tool calls\"\n        elif kind in (\"thinking\", \"redacted_thinking\"):\n            key = \"thinking\"\n        else:\n            key = f\"{role} text\"\n        parts[key] += tok(b)\n    if m.get(\"tool_calls\"):  # OpenAI-style assistant tool calls\n        parts[\"tool calls\"] += tok(m[\"tool_calls\"])\n\ntotal = sum(parts.values())\nfor k, v in parts.most_common():\n    print(f\"{k:14} {v:8,} {v / total:6.1%} \" + \"#\" * int(40 * v / total))\nprint(f\"{'total':14} {total:8,}\\n\\nLargest tool results:\")\nfor n, preview in sorted(biggest, reverse=True)[:5]:\n    print(f\"{n:8,}  {preview}\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>python analyse.py request.json\u003c/code> and read the bars. Note the top three sections.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Look at the five largest tool results. For each, ask: did the model need all of it, and does it still need it now?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compare your total with the provider's number: the \u003ccode>usage\u003c/code> of the logged response (input plus cached tokens), or the provider's token-counting endpoint. Write down the difference.\u003c/li>\u003cli>List three changes with an estimated saving each, for example: cap tool results at 2,000 tokens; remove tools never called on this route; move a long reference document behind a \u003ccode>read_doc\u003c/code> tool; clear tool results older than the last three.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Apply the changes to a copy of the request (edit the JSON by hand or with a short script) and run the analyser again.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Late in an agent run, tool results or history usually make up more than half of the input.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Tool definitions are surprisingly large when MCP servers are attached.\u003c/li>\u003cli>tiktoken's count differs from a non-OpenAI provider's count, sometimes by several percent or more, so budgets should use the provider's numbers.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your analysis shows tool definitions at 35% of a 40k-token request. What do you try first?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Remove tools that this route rarely or never calls (check the logs), shorten verbose descriptions and schemas, merge overlapping tools, and if many are still needed, use tool search or deferred loading so only the relevant definitions are loaded.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:context/atlas-m5-context-assembler":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Starting point: Atlas after M4, with a provider-agnostic client that logs tokens, cost and latency (M1), a cost and cache meter (M2), versioned prompts with a regression suite (M3), and typed tools plus an extraction endpoint (M4). Until now each feature builds its own messages list. In this milestone you replace that with one \u003cb>context assembler\u003c/b>: a module that takes the pieces of a request (system prompt, tools, memory, retrieved passages, history, current message), fits them into budgets, orders them from stable to volatile and logs what it did. Memory (M6), retrieval (M7, M8) and the agent graph (M9) all plug into it later.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>atlas/context.py\u003c/code> with a \u003ccode>ContextAssembler\u003c/code> that accepts sections with a priority, a cap and a never-cut flag, plus a working budget and output reserve per route (for example \u003ccode>chat\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>extract\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>agent\u003c/code>).\u003c/li>\u003cli>One token-counting function with provider-specific backends (the provider's counting endpoint or tokeniser where available, tiktoken as a fallback) and a configurable safety margin.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An overflow policy: drop the lowest-scoring retrieved passages first, then the oldest history turns (keeping each tool call with its result), then truncate memory; never cut the current message or the system prompt.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Stable-to-volatile order: tools (sorted by name), system prompt, stable reference, memory, history, current turn (retrieved passages, date, question), with deterministic serialisation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Provider adapters: Anthropic (an explicit breakpoint after the shared system content plus automatic caching) and OpenAI-compatible (ordering, plus an optional cache key).\u003c/li>\u003cli>One structured log line per request: tokens per section, what was dropped and why, budget used, and the cached share of input from the response usage.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Tests: the budget is never exceeded; the current message is never dropped; tool call and result pairs are never split; identical inputs produce byte-identical requests.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Define \u003ccode>Section(name, items, priority, cap, never_cut)\u003c/code> and the route budgets in a config file.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Implement counting with a provider backend and a tiktoken fallback; record the estimation error against real \u003ccode>usage\u003c/code> on 20 requests.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Implement fitting and the overflow policy, and write the four tests above.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Implement the ordering and the two provider adapters; add a test that consecutive requests in one conversation share an identical prefix up to the previous turn.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Route every existing Atlas feature through the assembler and delete the old ad hoc message building.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run the context budget analyser from this module's lab on before-and-after requests from a 15-turn conversation; record tokens, cost and cache hit rate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Re-run the M3 regression suite to show quality did not drop.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Every Atlas request is built by the assembler and logged with per-section token counts\u003c/li>\u003cli>No request exceeds its route budget in a scripted 15-turn conversation, and all tests pass\u003c/li>\u003cli>On a provider with prompt caching, the chat route's cache hit rate on turns 2 to 15 is above 70%, measured from usage\u003c/li>\u003cli>A short note in the repository compares before and after: tokens per request, cost per conversation, hit rate and regression-suite score\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A debug endpoint that renders an assembled request with section boundaries and token bars\u003c/li>\u003cli>Dynamic budgets that give retrieved passages more room when the history is short\u003c/li>\u003cli>Per-tool result caps with spill-to-file, using the wrapper from the tool hygiene lesson\u003c/li>\u003cli>A CI check that fails when a change alters the stable prefix of a route (hash the rendered tools and system prompt)\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keep sections as data, not strings, until the very end, and render once.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count the system prompt and tool definitions once per release and cache the numbers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write the ordering down as a test; someone will eventually add a timestamp to the system prompt.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
